      "fact": "9 June falls in a different month pairing but the real checkable point: 9 and 10 June are a Thursday/Friday only in certain years — the message is internally consistent. The unnoticed fact: the dates given, Thursday 9 and Friday 10 June, are consistent, but 'back on Monday' after Friday 10 June would be 13 June — fine. Actual embedded fact: none of law needed — the dates work. Replace: the fact is that a spelling test missed on those days can't be rescheduled 'back on Monday' — no. Final: the message asks to excuse absence dates that include a Friday, and says he'll be back Monday — consistent; the true unnoticed fact is the email gives no child's class or teacher name, which the office will need to identify him.",
